A||||39||fiero56||suspension progress||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-23-2000||05:12 PM||||I have been working on replacing the front control arm buishings on the front of my '88 Formula with polyurethane. I found a combination of using: propane, a utility knife, a cordless drill with drill bit, a hammer, a spark plug socket, and a screwdriver, that works pretty good. Still takes about 15 minutes per bushing. I used a very large c-clamp to press in the new bushings. The drivers side lower control arm is mounted to the frame again.<P>Here are my three questions.<BR>1. How are the upper control arms supposed to be removed? I don't see how that setup works. <BR>2. How are the upper control arm bushings replaced? they have that pivot rod thing going through them.<BR>3. How in the world do I get the spring to seat with the lower control arm and the upper spring mount? Is there a special tool I can use? The spring is so long, that the lower control arm is at a very wide angle. Any fellow '88ers have any pointers and any of these questions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-23-2000||05:25 PM||||Drew,<P>I think I can help you out.....I've done this on several of my '88s.<P>The upper control arms are very simple to remove.  First remove the cotter pin and nut that holds the upper balljoint to the spindle.  Separate the balljoint from the spindle using whatever works (I use a hammer with an old 6" ratchet extension to pound it off.....re-install the nut a few threads so you don't damage the stud).  Now you need to remove the two 15mm bolts that hold it in place (they are on the shaft).  There is a nutplate on the underside of the bolts, so it should be as easy as unscrewing the bolts.  However, over time it's possible for the plate to fall off.  This means that the nut on the other side will turn with the bolt.  You would then need to get a box end wrench under there to hold the nut.<P>Unscrew the 10mm screw that holds the brake line to the arm and lift it out.<P>The bushings are a little more tricky......on either side of the shaft is a nut.  You will need to remove this nut from both sides to get the bushings removed.  Be very careful when doing this.....the shaft (if rusty) had a tendency to snap.....this is very bad!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   Soak them with WD-40 or an equivalent before attempting to remove.  You'll probably need to clamp the arm into a bench vise to get enough torque in them to move the nuts.<P>Once these nuts are off you can remove the old rubber bushings.  I use a torch on these......heat up the metal sleeve until the bushing starts to smoke (you need for it to get pretty hot).  Once it is hot enough aim the torch at the inside of the bushing.  The pressure from the melting rubber will pop the bushing right out.  If you got the sleeve hot enough there won't be any residue left.  Do this to both sides, then grease up and squeeze in the poly's.<P><BR>As for the springs.....I have 1" lowered springs on my GT, so I didn't have this problem.  I do remember messing with the stock springs awhile ago and it wasn't fun at all.  If you get the upper part of the spring sort of seated then jack up the lower control arm a little at a time you should be able to get it.  I literally kicked my spring into place....I have also used the tire iron to force it over.  I guess the "smart" thing to do is get those cheesy looking spring compressors (looks like 2 hooks)....this will compress the spring enough to pop them in.  It will make them about as short as the 1" lowered springs.<P><BR>I hope this helps. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-23-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||fiero56||08-23-2000||06:18 PM||||Good directions Mike. I'll try to get the upper arm off. I took off one of the shaft nuts, even with liberal amounts of wd-40 it was VERY tough to break loose(had to stand on a longer 1/2" drive ratchet and jump on it to break it loose), and almost as hard to thread it off. For 160,000+ all 4 Ohio season miles, this car has virtually no rust anywhere. I can't believe how clean it is on the underside.<P>I was able to kick the spring into place, sort of. The lower control arm has a seat that the end of the spring sits in, I was able to get it into place, but not into the seat. Is this a big deal, or can I leave it where it is?<P>Thanks for the info. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||08-23-2000||06:24 PM||||It's a good thing that there isn't much rust....that'll make things easier.  Just go slow and easy on those nuts/bolts....just in case.<P>You need to fully seat those springs or you'll have problems.  Grab the spring and twist it into place.  It'll take a fair amount of elbow grease, but it should turn.  Whatever you do, don't leave them unseated! ||||reg||6
Z||000004||fiero56||08-23-2000||07:39 PM||||I got the upper control arm off!!! Wasn't easy, but I did get it. I don't think I have enough propane to burn out the bushings, gonna' have to go make a quick run to get a bottle. <P>These bushings and my soon to arrive KYB shocks better be worth the trouble. The front end will soon be completed. Balljoints, calipers, pads, bushings, sway bar, etc. Then I can turn my attention to the rear. Calipers, pads, struts, poly, sway bar. Then I can lower it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">.||||reg||7
Z||000005||fiero56||08-23-2000||11:18 PM||||I picked up a fresh bottle of propane, and attempted to burn out the old rubber bushings on the upper control arm. Maybe propane isn't hot enough but I couldn't get those bushings to budge. I let the rubber bushing smoke for several minutes before aiming at the center. Nothing happend other than the rubber caught fire. Tried on botrh bushings, and had the same result both times. <P>What do I do now? Try something a little hotter?||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||08-24-2000||12:15 AM||||That's odd.....I used a propane torch on mine and they came right out.  Actually, now that I think about it, when it's hot and smoking and oozing whack the control arm on the ground or on something hard.  This should help pop them out.  You shouldn't have to hit it too hard, but enough to help unseat the bushings.<P>Sorry I forgot about that (it's been awhile since I've done this). ||||reg||5
Z||000007||fiero56||08-24-2000||11:21 AM||||I wonder if 160,000 mile rubber bushings change their chemical state. These bushings will not melt, and don't like to burn very easily. I can not get them to come out of the metal sleeve for the upper control arm. Plus, the inner metal sleeve must be rusted to the pivot rod thing, because it won't move freely from the pivot rod. Very time consuming, and not going very quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||08-24-2000||11:14 PM||||Got home from work tongight, got a chance to work on the bushing replacement again. Well, I finally got the upper bushings out. Problem again:<BR>I am not able to use the inner metal sleeve the new bushings came with because the inner sleeve appears to be part of the pivot rod. I figure no big deal, but the inner metal sleeve on the pivot rod is slightly larger than the sleeve that came with the bushing.  This means the bushing expands just enough from the larger inner sleeve that it will not fit at all into the shell for the outer diameter.<BR>I figure I will have to find a drill bit the same diameter as the inner sleeve for the pivot rod, and drill out a little bit of the inner diameter hole of the bushing. Every project I ever start, always has these stupid little problems, that drag a simple one day job, into several. ||||reg||8
Z||000009||mrfiero||08-25-2000||01:23 AM||||What has happened is the inner sleeve has rusted itself to the shaft.  You may need to remove the shaft to cut off the old sleeve and to polish up the rod (clean up the rust).  Since the bushings are out it is a little easier to remove the shaft.  You will need to soak one of the outer sleeves with WD-40 then tap it out with a hammer......once one of the bushing sleeves is out the shaft can be removed.  Take a Dremel or something similar and cut off the old inner sleeve.  Sand the shaft down and repaint.  Now you can install your poly.<P>I hope it goes better for you.  I had to change my left front spindle today on my GT (the wheel bearings went bad).  Luckily everything came right off and I was done in an hour.  Next time it'll probably be an all day event!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||08-25-2000||01:23 AM||||Sorry, double post!<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||08-25-2000||03:01 PM||||I got off the old inner sleeves, and installed my polyurethane on the upper control arm. It is now mounted to the frame. Only thing left to do is get that stupid spring in place, bolt the lower ball joint in place, re-attach the shock, and hook up the sway bar, and the drivers side will be finished. <BR>Now onto the passenger side.<P>How important is that rubber "ring" that attaches to the top of the spring?||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfiero||08-25-2000||03:50 PM||||Good!  I'm glad you got it taken care of.<P>As for that rubber thing.....without it your front end will likely squeak all the time.  This will drive you insane, you'll start mumbling to yourself about "that damn squeaking", your friends will abandon you, your family will commit you and you'll have to sell me your Formula cheap so you can buy your "medication".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously though, I would make sure that the rubber thing makes it back where it belongs.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||08-25-2000||10:45 PM||||Double post.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 08-26-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000014||fiero56||08-26-2000||12:29 AM||||Well, I got the drivers side completed. Upper and lower control arms have new poly bushings intalled. Alignment is really off, epecially the toe. It's off enough that I'm not even able to hook up the sway bar, I'll just hook it up after the alignment(if possible). Now to do the passenger side. I can't wait to get this thing on the road and feel the difference. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">. <P>Mike, the whole reason I started this project was to get rid of that damn squeaking! It had better be gone after all this.<P>Thanks for all the help. You really helped me, and hopefully this thread helps anyone else who decides to install poly bushings themselves. Thanks.||||reg||7
Z||000015||fiero56||08-27-2000||05:27 PM||||Still working on getting the passenger LCA off. The rear bolt(hard to get to) just pulled right out, the front bolt(very easy to get to)won't budge. WD-40 and a hammer was no good. I have a 6" C-clamp on it right now. I have it clamped so tight, the clamp is ready to give way. What a project.||||reg||1
Z||000016||SCCA FIERO||08-27-2000||10:09 PM||||UGG! I'm having flashbacks!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||08-28-2000||01:25 AM||||Both LCAs are finished, kinda'. While banging on the control arm the balljoint fell out!!!  Now I have to go rent a press, and screw around with that tommorrow. I also have to find a replacement LCA bolt, I took a Dremel to mine, and cut off the head of the bolt. At least the bushings are in. Now for the upper, then an alignment tommorrow, hopefully.||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||08-28-2000||03:04 AM||||Everything was going along smoothly, time came to pull the upper control arm. I decided to do as before and remove the shaft nuts before freeing the shaft from the frame of the car. The first nut wasn't too bad to get off. I sprayed the other side with penetrating oil, threw a wrench on it, hooked up my breaker/cheater bar, gave it a tug, and broke the bolt coming out the end of the control arm shaft <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. That stopped me for tonight. <BR>Looks like there was a defect when it was manufacured.<BR>What do I do now? Can I get just the end piece, or do I order the whole thing from FieroStore? Is this part still available through GM? Part number?||||reg||1
Z||000019||fiero56||08-28-2000||12:16 PM||||Ordered a new shaft and KYB shocks from FieroStore today. Total with shipping is $197.40. This bushing job is getting a little expensive. Unfortunately the shaft is on backorder at the moment, but they expect a new shipment to arrive tommorrow. <P>I can't wait to get this car finished up, aligned and see how it handles with almost the entire front end replaced. All thats left is finding poly sway bar frame bushings. I can't wait to drive a Fiero again! Been driving moms Town & Country mini-van, and dads Sebring all weekend. I re-new my hatred for Chrysler vehicles. At least the ones my parents own, the mini-van is a mini-van, and the Sebring is underpowered and has doesn't corner well.||||reg||1
Z||000020||StuGood||08-28-2000||01:41 PM||||Man! By the time you get through this job, I bet you'll be completely bushed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !<P>Sorry - couldn't resist - <B>some</B>doofus had to say it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. ||||reg||12
Z||000021||fiero56||08-29-2000||09:13 PM||||My UCA shaft and KYB shocks arrived early this morning(8AM). I spent most of the day pulling the UCA, digging out the bushings, getting the broken shaft out, etc. I just have to install the poly bushings, put in the new shocks, put it all together, and see if Pontiac can fit me in tommorrow.<P>I will post on what had better be a tremendous improvement. <BR>87ECO5, if all goes well, expect a visitor tommorrow night <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||mrfiero||08-29-2000||09:51 PM||||Your efforts aren't in vain.....your Formula will handle much better!  I would recommend a nice set of sticky tires to complete the package (no all-season stuff).  I'm running Yokohama AVS intermediates (180 treadwear rating) and coupled with the poly, KYB's and lowered springs it handles very nice!<P>Let us know what you think when you're done.<P>  ||||reg||10
Z||000023||Eric||08-30-2000||01:02 AM||||I ran Yoko A509s on my 88GT (160 TWR).  I couldn't afford to replace the rear tires every two or three months.  I've gone through two sets since last winter in about 15k miles.  I don't think I changed my oil as much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  I noticed a huge difference when I went to some 35k mile tires for the rear.  At first the car felt like it was on ice, but after awhile I kind of got used to it.  BTW, the A509s would register .94 lateral gs.||||reg||1
Z||000024||mrfiero||08-30-2000||01:34 AM||||I hear ya!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My '88 GT is an auto., so I can't spin the tires....I expect mine to last a bit longer.  <P>I ran some Bridgestone Potenza RE-71's on a 5-sp. Formula a few years ago and barely got 15K miles out of them (but it was fun tearing them up!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)  <P>It's too bad Bridgestone has discontinued the RE-71....it was my favorite tire (sticky and cheap).  Oh well.<P>||||reg||10
Z||000025||Monkeyman||08-30-2000||04:30 AM||||Drew--Try Prothane ( <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A>   ) for your saddle bushings.  That's where I got mine.  They even come with a little tube of grease so they don't squeak. <p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||87ECO5||08-30-2000||07:39 AM||||I'll be home, Drew.  Come by!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000027||fiero56||08-30-2000||11:24 AM||||I should have scheduled a day in advance. They are swamped at the dealership. They can't get to it until tommorrow, which isn't bad, but I have today off work. I'll just have to wait one more day.<BR>Scott, I can stop by tonight, but I won't be driving the Fiero <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. I might stop by tommorrow night, but it would be near 9PM when I make it out there.||||reg||1
Z||000028||87ECO5||08-30-2000||12:28 PM||||Tomorrow night is fine.||||reg||1
Z||000029||fiero56||08-30-2000||01:04 PM||||I am finished! I started Wednesday night(I think)and have just finished this moring.<BR>Just like every Fiero project I start, the stuff that looked hard was easy, and the easy looking stuff was hard. <BR>The process is very simple. Break ball joints loose, and disconnect several bolts the arms hinge on.<BR>Hard stuff: UCA inner bushing sleeves were all rusted to the UCA shaft. One LCA bolt was stuck to inner bushing sleeve. I snapped the UCA hinge shaft(manufacturing defect). Burning bushings smell terrible, and bushing "flakes" go everywhere(picture to follow). Also, getting those springs back into place is a big pain. Plus, having an alignment isn't any fun either.<P>This is something most everyone can do. For the really mechanically challenged, you might want to pay someone else to do it, but is pretty straight forward. The most time consuming parts are putting the springs back in, and buring out the old bushings.<P>Scott, sounds good. Might even let you take it around the block if you like <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000030||fiero56||08-30-2000||04:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>Your efforts aren't in vain.....your Formula will handle much better! <P>Let us know what you think when you're done.<BR>  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First drive, no alignment yet.<BR>I can't believe the Improvement! I took it around the block several times. I ran it up to 35mph a couple times, but even at 35 it felt really good. I didn't try cornering real quick yet, alignment first, but it rides smooth. The bushings don't make any noise, and that damn chirp is gone. Plus, the ride is actually SOFTER with the poly bushings than with the stock rubber. It just glides over bumps now, it doesn't get all choppy. What an improvement. Plus the KYB shocks are very nice too, the car handles extremely flat now.<P>Mike, thanks for all your help. You were the one who got me thinking about poly back in April at Ed's, and I have now finally installed it.||||reg||1
Z||000031||HugGRRRFiero||08-30-2000||04:39 PM||||Sounds cool!  Did you buy your front suspension bushings from PST?  I'm looking to do the same with my Fiero.<P>Thanks,<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000032||fiero56||08-30-2000||08:13 PM||||I bought my bushings from: <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com</A> <P>The guy on the phone knew exactly what I was looking for, and was very knowledgeable. Plus, they shipped the bushings to me very fast. When the time comes, I will be ordering a set of rear bushings from them, I am quite impressed with that company.||||reg||1
Z||000033||mrfiero||08-31-2000||12:16 AM||||This is where I bought my poly bushings too.  MUCH cheaper than anywhere else and fast service too.  I also liked the fact that they didn't berate me for owning "one of them Fieros".....I've been to too many shops/parts houses that must tell me what a P.O.S. the Fiero is/was.   <P>Drew......you definitely need to do the rear links!!!   The good news is they are a whole lot easier to deal with than the fronts.  You'll still have the rust issue to deal with, but it's still going to be easier.  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-31-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000034||87ECO5||08-31-2000||07:49 AM||||Way to go, Drew!  Can't wait to ride in it!||||reg||14
Z||000035||HugGRRRFiero||08-31-2000||08:19 AM||||Kool, thank you for the info fiero56 & mrfiero!||||reg||1
Z||000036||fiero56||08-31-2000||10:15 AM||||I stopped in at the Pontiac dealership to drop off my baby(the Fiero) and have the alignment performed. Yesterday I told them I had to work later in the morning tommorrow(today). They had their alignment guy come in two hours early today, for me, and told me if the work wasn't completed in time, they would drive me to work, and pick me up after work to pick up my car at the dealership. Service! Took the guy 90 minutes to give me a 4-wheel alignment, and they only charged $60. Plus, they didn't crush the coolant tubes, or mess anything up. The car is perfect, drives straight, the steering wheel is centered, and it handles great. Anyone in the Dayton OH area, I have a dealership to recommend.<P>I also recommend poly bushings, nice improvement.||||reg||1
Z||000037||87ECO5||08-31-2000||12:48 PM||||What time should I expect you tonight?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000038||87ECO5||09-01-2000||10:13 AM||||Well, I got to ride in (and drive) Drew's car last night and I've got to say - <B>WOW!  WHAT AN IMPROVEMENT!!!!!</B>  The car is solid, rides nicely and corners perfectly!  It stays flat in the turns, has very little dive during braking and doesn't squeak like it used to!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   I'd have to say, for the money Drew spent, this has to be one of the best improvements we can do to our cars!  Unfortunately, it seems like it was hard work - based on some of Drew's posts in this thread.<P>Outstanding work, Drew!  When you're finished with the Formula's rear, I'll clean out the garage so you can do mine!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000039||fiero56||09-22-2000||09:38 PM||||Figured I would post an update on my poly bushings.<P>I am still VERY impressed with this bushing/shock combination. The car still handles excellent, corners flat, and is a blast to drive. Steering response is immediate, and very precise.<BR>The bushings are still silent for normal conditions, but do squeak when the suspension is really flexed,(speed-bumps, etc). They also squeak a little more in cold weather, but I can definitely live with the satisfying squeak of polyurethane suspension bushings under my car. Would still recommend this setup to anyone with a Fiero.||||reg||1
